WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.501 --> 00:00:03.167
-Our first guest tonight
is a talented comedian, actor,

00:00:03.167 --> 00:00:05.501
and writer you know from his
work on "Saturday Night Live,"

00:00:05.501 --> 00:00:06.834
"Tough Crowd,"
and "Train Wreck."

00:00:06.834 --> 00:00:08.734
His new show "Small Talk"
is playing

00:00:08.734 --> 00:00:10.734
at the Lucille Lortel Theatre
here in New York.

00:00:10.734 --> 00:00:12.133
Please welcome back to the show
our friend

00:00:12.133 --> 00:00:13.901
Colin Quinn, everybody!

00:00:13.901 --> 00:00:15.934
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:15.934 --> 00:00:28.400
♪♪

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:30.567
-Hello!
-Hey!

00:00:30.567 --> 00:00:32.467
Hey, I don't want to start off
with complaints,

00:00:32.467 --> 00:00:33.968
but, you know...

00:00:33.968 --> 00:00:35.801
[ Laughter ]

00:00:35.801 --> 00:00:37.467
Maybe a nice cup,
a Seth Meyers cup?

00:00:37.467 --> 00:00:39.133
-Oh, you want a cup?
-This is really cheese ball.

00:00:39.133 --> 00:00:40.467
-I'm sorry.

00:00:40.467 --> 00:00:41.901
Well, you had
a nice cup backstage.

00:00:41.901 --> 00:00:43.234
Why'd you come out here --
-Now I look weird.

00:00:43.234 --> 00:00:44.901
-Why do you want water
right away?

00:00:44.901 --> 00:00:48.067
-Because I like to lubricate
my throat, you know, for the...

00:00:48.067 --> 00:00:50.067
I'm a -- You know,
it's a professional thing.

00:00:50.067 --> 00:00:51.567
In the theater.
-You're a theater guy?

00:00:51.567 --> 00:00:52.934
-I'm a theater guy, yeah.

00:00:52.934 --> 00:00:54.434
-Now, your show is about
small talk.

00:00:54.434 --> 00:00:55.534
-Yes.
-What is some...

00:00:55.534 --> 00:00:57.033
Was that small talk
we just had there?

00:00:57.033 --> 00:00:58.968
What is some good small talk?
-No, that was complaints.

00:00:58.968 --> 00:01:01.033
Complaining is not small talk.
-Okay.

00:01:01.033 --> 00:01:02.534
-You didn't come in hot
like that.

00:01:02.534 --> 00:01:04.033
-Okay.
-It's gotta be more positive.

00:01:04.033 --> 00:01:06.434
-So, what's good opening
positive small talk?

00:01:06.434 --> 00:01:08.000
-Like, well, you know,
the best...

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:10.234
One of the best ones,
of course today --

00:01:10.234 --> 00:01:12.067
the weather, the rain.

00:01:12.067 --> 00:01:15.167
Rain is like the, you know,
all-star small-talk stuff,

00:01:15.167 --> 00:01:16.701
'cause it's always shocking,

00:01:16.701 --> 00:01:18.501
even after all these years
on the planet.

00:01:18.501 --> 00:01:20.667
Every time it rains, everybody's
like, "You believe this?"

00:01:20.667 --> 00:01:21.667
[ Laughter ]

00:01:21.667 --> 00:01:23.000
"What's going on?"

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:25.167
And then I can say,
"You look good."

00:01:25.167 --> 00:01:26.834
And, you know, even if you
think I'm being fake --

00:01:26.834 --> 00:01:29.000
But you do look good,
obviously you're working out,

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:30.367
and then, you know, you go,
"You're doing good,

00:01:30.367 --> 00:01:32.167
you're looking good,
good for you."

00:01:32.167 --> 00:01:34.434
And then you just kinda
put it on that person.

00:01:34.434 --> 00:01:36.567
And you know, depends on
how you want to...

00:01:36.567 --> 00:01:38.400
You try to read --
like, I try to read

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:40.234
if you want to complain,

00:01:40.234 --> 00:01:43.234
you know, if I'm like, "I mean,
geez, what's going on?"

00:01:43.234 --> 00:01:45.300
you know, or if you want
to be reassured, like,

00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:46.968
"It'll work itself out."

00:01:46.968 --> 00:01:48.167
You know, just kinda go.
-This is all very --

00:01:48.167 --> 00:01:49.667
You're a natural.
You're a natural at this.

00:01:49.667 --> 00:01:51.667
-Yes, I was born to do it.
-Now, I should say --

00:01:51.667 --> 00:01:54.000
small talk, very different
from talk-show talk.

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:55.167
-Yes.
-Talk-show talk, I mean,

00:01:55.167 --> 00:01:56.634
is a very unique skill.

00:01:56.634 --> 00:01:59.133
-Talk-show talk, you have to,
like, come out with the story.

00:01:59.133 --> 00:02:01.234
-Yeah.
-And so you can't small talk.

00:02:01.234 --> 00:02:04.400
If you're in the elevator,
even if it's a big star,

00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:06.667
and they can't just talk
to strangers and go,

00:02:06.667 --> 00:02:09.000
"Oh, my God, wait till you
hear what David O. Russell,

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:11.167
on the set -- this is
actually very funny..."

00:02:11.167 --> 00:02:13.334
[ Laughter ]

00:02:13.334 --> 00:02:15.467
By the way, that's my most
modern reference.

00:02:15.467 --> 00:02:17.801
David O. Russell. Sorry, folks.
-David O. Russell, very modern.

00:02:17.801 --> 00:02:19.133
-A little behind the times.

00:02:19.133 --> 00:02:20.467
-But this is very...

00:02:20.467 --> 00:02:23.634
I was surprised to hear
that you like small talk.

00:02:23.634 --> 00:02:25.100
I would have guessed,
if you'd asked,

00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:27.133
that you were not a fan of it.
-I know that's how people think.

00:02:27.133 --> 00:02:29.801
But what bothers me is people
that move to New York

00:02:29.801 --> 00:02:32.300
and then go, "Oh, I moved here
so I don't have to be friendly."

00:02:32.300 --> 00:02:35.767
It's like, um, I think you
meant to move to the woods.

00:02:35.767 --> 00:02:39.234
Because this is a big city with
millions of people.

00:02:39.234 --> 00:02:40.367
You have to be...

00:02:40.367 --> 00:02:42.200
You're gonna have
interactions with people.

00:02:42.200 --> 00:02:43.534
-And you get excited about --

00:02:43.534 --> 00:02:46.767
Now, how short can small talk be
before it's...

00:02:46.767 --> 00:02:49.067
I mean, I guess how long is it
when it's no longer small talk?

00:02:49.067 --> 00:02:50.434
-Well, yeah, that's the thing,
is leaving.

00:02:50.434 --> 00:02:51.601
You know what I mean?
-Yeah.

00:02:51.601 --> 00:02:53.434
-Leaving the small talk
conversation.

00:02:53.434 --> 00:02:55.100
Some people go too long.

00:02:55.100 --> 00:02:56.267
You know, I'm talking
about the weather,

00:02:56.267 --> 00:02:57.367
somebody in the elevator,
I'm like,

00:02:57.367 --> 00:02:58.701
"Boy, weather's crazy."

00:02:58.701 --> 00:03:01.033
They're like, "Yeah, yeah,
yesterday was...

00:03:01.033 --> 00:03:02.367
Next week's supposed to be
even crazier."

00:03:02.367 --> 00:03:05.367
You're like, "Yeah."
"Last week, Tuesday was..."

00:03:05.367 --> 00:03:08.033
And then suddenly you're
pressing all the buttons.

00:03:08.033 --> 00:03:10.367
You can't escape.
It's like social media.

00:03:10.367 --> 00:03:12.501
-Yeah, but technology has
actually kind of helped

00:03:12.501 --> 00:03:14.334
create an escape route for it.

00:03:14.334 --> 00:03:15.767
-Yeah, the uncomfortable...

00:03:15.767 --> 00:03:17.934
When you're done, you ever like
to unwind at Starbucks,

00:03:17.934 --> 00:03:19.300
and you talk
and then you're like,

00:03:19.300 --> 00:03:21.133
"Oh, God, there's nothing left
to say to this person."

00:03:21.133 --> 00:03:22.801
And you sort of
half know them,

00:03:22.801 --> 00:03:25.634
and you're just both staring
and you just feel terrible.

00:03:25.634 --> 00:03:27.133
You bust out the phone.

00:03:27.133 --> 00:03:28.300
You know what I mean?
-Yeah.

00:03:28.300 --> 00:03:29.567
-In the old days,
you didn't have the phone.

00:03:29.567 --> 00:03:30.734
You'd have to whistle, I guess.

00:03:30.734 --> 00:03:32.434
I don't know
what they were doing.

00:03:32.434 --> 00:03:34.434
That's gonna be talk shows.

00:03:34.434 --> 00:03:36.200
In five years, this will be
every talk show.

00:03:36.200 --> 00:03:37.367
What's up, Seth?
How you doing?

00:03:37.367 --> 00:03:39.200
-Not much, how are you?
-Good, good.

00:03:39.200 --> 00:03:41.534
Show's doing good.
-Yeah, and you got a new show

00:03:41.534 --> 00:03:42.801
at the Lucille Lortel.

00:03:42.801 --> 00:03:44.300
That's great.
-Yeah, yeah. Hold on.

00:03:44.300 --> 00:03:46.300
[ Laughter ]

00:03:46.300 --> 00:03:50.133
-Now, you think that we are
making a mistake as a society

00:03:50.133 --> 00:03:52.634
because we're not teaching
small talk to our children.

00:03:52.634 --> 00:03:55.801
-Yes, I feel like
we're the personality country.

00:03:55.801 --> 00:03:58.601
That was our whole thing --
talk, free speech.

00:03:58.601 --> 00:04:01.000
And yet kids get shoved
into school like,

00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:02.834
"Study hard and
you'll be a success."

00:04:02.834 --> 00:04:04.334
Is that really...

00:04:04.334 --> 00:04:06.067
In this country, either
you can count really high

00:04:06.067 --> 00:04:07.567
or you better have
a great personality.

00:04:07.567 --> 00:04:08.968
[ Laughter ]

00:04:08.968 --> 00:04:12.133
And yeah, so kids should be
walking in, you teach them like,

00:04:12.133 --> 00:04:13.367
this is the big leagues.

00:04:13.367 --> 00:04:14.868
You're going into society.

00:04:14.868 --> 00:04:17.701
The rest of your life's gonna be
here, not with your family,

00:04:17.701 --> 00:04:19.367
so you'd better
get it going, you know?

00:04:19.367 --> 00:04:22.234
Walk in, and just --
just memorize,

00:04:22.234 --> 00:04:24.200
rehearse a couple of phrases,

00:04:24.200 --> 00:04:26.033
not surefire, but close.

00:04:26.033 --> 00:04:28.000
You know, like, teach a kid
first day of preschool.

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:30.801
Walk in, "Is it me or is this
bus driver a little off?"

00:04:30.801 --> 00:04:32.467
[ Laughter ]

00:04:32.467 --> 00:04:34.801
You know, kids will be like,
"I like that kid."

00:04:34.801 --> 00:04:37.100
You know, just have
a little story.

00:04:37.100 --> 00:04:38.267
-Right, so right away,
the other kids are like --

00:04:38.267 --> 00:04:39.501
-Be like, "Waving goodbye
to my Mom,

00:04:39.501 --> 00:04:41.000
and you guys shut the doors
in my face.

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:42.334
You don't do that."

00:04:42.334 --> 00:04:45.667
Work the crowd -- "You get it.
This kid gets it."

00:04:45.667 --> 00:04:47.868
-So you think I should
teach my kids to say,

00:04:47.868 --> 00:04:49.334
"You get it, this kid gets it"?

00:04:49.334 --> 00:04:50.834
[ Laughter ]
-I think that's...

00:04:50.834 --> 00:04:53.334
You teach them that,
and you teach them this.

00:04:53.334 --> 00:04:56.000
If you want them to be
successful -- this.

00:04:56.000 --> 00:04:58.167
When somebody's talking to you,
this works.

00:04:58.167 --> 00:04:59.501
[ Light laughter ]
-Yeah.

00:04:59.501 --> 00:05:02.734
-Absolutely tremendous, yes.

00:05:02.734 --> 00:05:05.133
No matter what they say,
just say, "Yeah, good point."

00:05:05.133 --> 00:05:07.133
And this --
people that go like this

00:05:07.133 --> 00:05:10.000
make $40,000 a year more
than people that go like this.

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:11.501
-Just to nod along
while people are talking.

00:05:11.501 --> 00:05:12.634
Nobody likes this.
-What if right now you were

00:05:12.634 --> 00:05:14.100
talking to me
and I was going like this?

00:05:14.100 --> 00:05:16.100
You'd be like, "I don't want him
back on the show."

00:05:16.100 --> 00:05:17.767
You go, "Yeah, yeah, yeah!"

00:05:17.767 --> 00:05:19.267
-Oh, I like this.
I like what you did there.

00:05:19.267 --> 00:05:21.300
-Yes, yes!
-But now, you feel like...

00:05:21.300 --> 00:05:22.667
This is tricky,
because you think

00:05:22.667 --> 00:05:26.000
we should teach animals
small talk, but obviously...

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:28.334
-No, that's a misconception.

00:05:28.334 --> 00:05:30.100
I'm just saying animals...

00:05:30.100 --> 00:05:32.100
Animals, because
they can't small talk,

00:05:32.100 --> 00:05:34.601
that's why they never
evolve past that level.

00:05:34.601 --> 00:05:37.267
You know, 'cause it's always
a staring contest.

00:05:37.267 --> 00:05:40.601
Even dogs, who you'd think would
know better by this point,

00:05:40.601 --> 00:05:42.767
hanging out with us
for thousands of years.

00:05:42.767 --> 00:05:44.501
They've still got that primal
thing

00:05:44.501 --> 00:05:45.801
because they can't break...

00:05:45.801 --> 00:05:47.300
It's like guys at a nightclub,

00:05:47.300 --> 00:05:49.133
like young guys
when the music's loud

00:05:49.133 --> 00:05:50.400
and they're just
staring at each other.

00:05:50.400 --> 00:05:52.067
It always turns into a fight.

00:05:52.067 --> 00:05:53.400
That's what dogs...

00:05:53.400 --> 00:05:55.067
Even when your dogs are,
like, pleasant,

00:05:55.067 --> 00:05:56.167
there's always that moment
you're like,

00:05:56.167 --> 00:05:58.667
"Oh, this is gonna go off,"
you know?

00:05:58.667 --> 00:06:01.400
-If dogs could small talk, what
sort of things do you think...

00:06:01.400 --> 00:06:03.467
Like, what would you want a dog
to be able to say to you?

00:06:03.467 --> 00:06:05.467
-Just, I don't know,
"I like your coat."

00:06:05.467 --> 00:06:07.634
"You look good." I don't know.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:07.634 --> 00:06:10.100
"Hey, did you see that rat?
Are you a terrier breed?

00:06:10.100 --> 00:06:11.968
You know, well, believe
me, in my breed,

00:06:11.968 --> 00:06:14.467
rats, we go after them."
And I go, "Didn't notice that."

00:06:14.467 --> 00:06:16.133
[ Laughter ]

00:06:16.133 --> 00:06:18.801
-Just breed details,
you'd want to hear explained.

00:06:18.801 --> 00:06:20.801
-Yeah, I feel like they would be
able to be like,

00:06:20.801 --> 00:06:23.300
"They think I'm this mixed
breed, but I'm actually this."

00:06:23.300 --> 00:06:26.634
Like, "We keep it down
in our family, but we're..."

00:06:26.634 --> 00:06:28.200
-I have a lot more
to ask you.

00:06:28.200 --> 00:06:30.701
We'll be right back with more
from Colin Quinn, everybody.

00:06:30.701 --> 00:06:32.133
[ Cheers and applause ]

